Restore from encrypted backup

Usage: evnx restore [OPTIONS] <BACKUP>

Arguments:
  <BACKUP>
          The backup file to decrypt, as written by `evnx backup`.
          
          A file on disk. For a vault in evnx cloud, use `evnx cloud pull`.

Options:
      --output <OUTPUT>
          Where to write the restored file.
          
          Defaults to the file the backup was made from — `.env.staging` for a backup of `.env.staging` — in the current folder. A recorded name that is not `.env` or `.env.<name>` restores to `.env`.
          
          An existing file is never replaced silently: evnx asks, or takes --yes, and keeps the replaced file as <output>.bak. Content that is not a .env evnx can read goes to <output>.restored instead (exit 5); that file is private (0600) and holds plaintext secrets.
          
          `-` prints the decrypted text to stdout, and every status line goes to stderr. Nothing is written, so there is no .bak and no .gitignore question. On a terminal it is refused unless --show-values.

      --show-values
          Print the decrypted text to a terminal anyway, with `--output -`.
          
          A value printed there stays in the scrollback. Piped, it is not needed.

  -y, --yes
          Replace an existing --output without asking.
          
          For scripts and CI, where there is no terminal to ask on. The file it replaces is kept beside it as <output>.bak (replacing the previous copy). Without it, and without a terminal, an existing output stops the run (exit 4).

      --gitignore
          Add the restored file to .gitignore without asking (no prompt)

      --no-gitignore
          Skip the .gitignore check for the restored file

  -n, --dry-run
          Decrypt and validate but do not write any files

      --inspect
          List variable names (never values) without writing any files.
          
          Decrypts the backup and prints each key name to stdout. Values are never displayed. No files are written and no overwrite prompt is shown. Use this to answer "what keys are in this backup?" before committing to a full restore.
          
          Example: evnx restore .env.backup --inspect

      --key-file <PATH>
          Read the passphrase from a file instead of prompting.
          
          The file should contain only the passphrase; leading and trailing whitespace, such as a final newline, is trimmed. Intended for CI/CD pipelines where interactive prompts are not possible.
          
          Security: less secure than the interactive prompt. The file path may appear in process listings and shell history. Use a secrets manager or a tmpfs-backed path (e.g. /run/secrets/) in production.
          
          EVNX_PASSWORD environment variable is also accepted and takes lower priority than --key-file; when both are set, the file wins and a warning says so.
          
          `evnx backup` takes the same flag, so one file round-trips.
          
          Examples: evnx restore .env.backup --key-file /run/secrets/evnx-pass EVNX_PASSWORD=mypass evnx restore .env.backup

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Replacing a file: an existing --output is never replaced silently. With a
terminal evnx asks; without one, pass --yes. Either way the
replaced file is kept beside it as <output>.bak. Content identical to the
backup is left alone.

Passphrase: --key-file, then EVNX_PASSWORD, then a prompt.

--output - prints the decrypted text to stdout, with every status line on
stderr; a terminal refuses it unless --show-values. Text that is not a .env
goes to <output>.restored instead: a private file (0600) that holds plaintext
secrets, printed with its full path.

A backup file is not a vault. For evnx cloud, use `evnx cloud pull` (one
version) or `evnx cloud export` (all of them).

Exit codes:
    0  Ran, nothing to report
    2  Could not run: usage, config, I/O, or refused before any change
    4  Declined: answered no, or a confirmation was needed with no terminal and no --yes
    5  restore wrote the result aside, to <output>.restored
  141  Output closed early (broken pipe)

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/restore
